### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**You should take the Standard Deduction.** 
For 2023, the standard deduction for a Single filer is **$13,850**. To itemize, your deductible expenses would need to exceed this amount. Your itemized deductions would primarily consist of your $650 charitable donation to Second Harvest Heartland and your State and Local Taxes (SALT) withheld from your W-2. Combined, these will fall well below $13,850, making the standard deduction much more beneficial for you.

### 2. Above-the-Line Deductions (Adjustments to Income)
Even if you take the standard deduction, you can still claim "above-the-line" deductions to lower your Adjusted Gross Income (AGI):
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You can deduct up to a maximum of **$2,500** in student loan interest. Since you paid $2,900 to OSLA, you will cap out at the $2,500 maximum. 
*   **Self-Employment Tax Deduction:** You will be able to deduct 50% of the self-employment tax you pay on your freelance income.

### 3. Freelance Deductions (1099-NEC)
Your $9,800 in freelance income is considered a small business by the IRS. You can deduct business expenses against this income to lower your taxable profit:
*   **Unreimbursed Work Supplies ($420):** If these supplies were for your W-2 job, you **cannot** deduct them (the IRS eliminated W-2 employee expenses in 2018). However, if they were for your freelance editing/proofreading work, you can deduct the full $420 as a business expense.
*   **Home Office Deduction:** 
    *   *The Exclusive Use Rule:* To claim this, the 180 sq ft room must be used **exclusively and regularly** for your freelance business. If you also use this room to work your full-time W-2 job, it fails the IRS "exclusive use" test, and you cannot claim the home office deduction. 
    *   *If it qualifies (used ONLY for freelance):* Your office is 22.22% of your apartment (180 / 810 sq ft). 
