*Note: Because you are taking the standard deduction, your $650 charitable donation will not provide a tax benefit this year (the special pandemic-era rule allowing non-itemizers to deduct charity expired).*

### 2. What You Can Deduct (Above-the-Line & Business Deductions)
Even with the standard deduction, you can take "above-the-line" deductions and business deductions:

*   **Student Loan Interest:** You paid $2,900, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500**. Additionally, because your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) will be around $80,000, you fall into the 2023 phase-out range for single filers ($75,000 to $90,000). This means your $2,500 maximum deduction will be partially reduced.
*   **Unreimbursed Work Supplies ($420):** 
    *   If these were for your W-2 job at Summit Financial: They are **not deductible**. The law currently prohibits W-2 employees from deducting unreimbursed employee expenses.
    *   If these were for your 1099-NEC freelance work: They are **fully deductible** as a business expense against your freelance income.
*   **Self-Employment Tax Deduction:** You get to deduct 50% of the self-employment tax you pay on your freelance income as an adjustment to your gross income.

### 3. How to Treat the Home Office
You can claim a home office deduction, but **only against your 1099-NEC freelance income**, not your W-2 job. Because you use the 180 sq ft room *exclusively* for work, it qualifies. You have two options:

*   **Option A: Simplified Method:** 180 sq ft × $5/sq ft = **$900 deduction**. (This is easy and requires minimal record-keeping).
*   **Option B: Actual Expenses Method:** Your office is 22.22% of your apartment's total square footage (180 ÷ 810). You can deduct 22.22% of your rent ($1,950 × 12 months = $23,400 × 22.22% = **$5,200**). You can also deduct 22.22% of your utilities, renter's insurance, and internet bill.
*   **Recommendation:** You should definitely use the **Actual Expenses Method**. It will give you a deduction of well over $5,200 (compared to $900), which will significantly lower your taxable freelance income and your self-employment tax.

### 4. Forms and Worksheets You'll Need
When you file, your return will need to include the following forms:
*   **Form 1040:** Your main individual tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your business income, your student loan interest deduction, and your self-employment tax deduction.
*   **Schedule 2:** To report your self-employment tax.
*   **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** You must file this because your taxable interest from Ally Bank ($1,880) is over the $1,500 threshold. (Your $380 Betterment dividends will also go here).
*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** To report your $9,800 in 1099-NEC income and deduct your work supplies (if freelance) and home office expenses.
*   **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** To calculate the Social Security and Medicare taxes on your net freelance income.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** You will need this form to calculate and claim your home office deduction using the Actual Expenses method.
*   **Student Loan Interest Deduction Worksheet:** Found in the Form 1040 instructions, you'll use this to calculate exactly how much of the $2,500 you can deduct after the MAGI phase-out.

