Based on the 2023 IRS tax rules, here is a complete breakdown of your situation, what you can deduct, and the forms you will need:

### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**You should definitely take the Standard Deduction.** 
For 2023, the standard deduction for a single filer is **$13,850**. Your only itemizable personal deduction is the $650 charitable donation to Second Harvest Heartland. Because $650 is far less than $13,850, itemizing would cause you to pay more in taxes. 

*(Note: Under current federal tax law, the $420 in unreimbursed work supplies is **not deductible** if it was for your W-2 job at Summit Financial. However, if those supplies were for your freelance editing work, you can deduct them as a business expense on Schedule C!)*

### 2. Freelance Income (1099-NEC) & Home Office
You will report your $9,800 freelance income as a sole proprietor. You can deduct expenses against this income to lower your taxable profit.

**Home Office Deduction:**
Since you use a 180 sq ft room exclusively for work out of an 810 sq ft apartment, your business use percentage is **22.22%** (180 ÷ 810). You have two ways to calculate this:
*   **Simplified Method:** 180 sq ft × $5/sq ft = **$900** deduction.
*   **Actual Expenses Method:** 22.22% of your annual rent ($1,950 × 12 months = $23,400) equals **$5,200**. You can also deduct 22.22% of your utilities, internet, and renter's insurance.
*   **Recommendation:** You should use the **Actual Expenses method**, as it provides a significantly larger deduction and will wipe out a large portion of your freelance tax liability.

### 3. Student Loan Interest Deduction
You paid $2,900 to OSLA, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500**. 
This is an "above-the-line" deduction, meaning you get to claim it even though you are taking the standard deduction. 

*Note on Phase-outs:* For 2023, this deduction begins to phase out for single filers with a Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) between $75,000 and $90,000. Your MAGI will be roughly $82,000 ($71k W-2 + ~$8k net freelance + $2.2k interest/dividends), so your $2,500 maximum deduction will be partially reduced.

### 4. Interest & Dividends
Your $1,880 from Ally Bank and $380 from Betterment total $2,260. Because your combined interest and dividend income is over the $1,500 threshold, you are required to report this on a specific schedule.

### 5. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When you file, make sure your software generates (or you manually fill out) the following forms:
*   **Form 1040:** Your main federal tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your net business income, your student loan interest deduction, and the deductible portion of your self-employment tax.
*   **Schedule 2:** To report your self-employment tax.
*   **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Required because your interest/dividends exceed $1,500.
*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** To report your $9,800 in 1099-NEC income and deduct your work supplies (if freelance) and home office expenses.
*   **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** To calculate the Social Security and Medicare taxes on your net freelance profit.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** Required to claim your home office deduction using the Actual Expenses method.
*   **Student Loan Interest Deduction Worksheet:** Found in the Form 1040 instructions to calculate exactly how much of the $2,500 you can deduct based on your MAGI phase-out.

*(Don't forget to file your Minnesota state return as well, which will start with your federal numbers but may have slight adjustments!)*

