Here is a breakdown of your tax situation for the 2023 tax year based on the details you provided. 

### 1. What You Can Deduct
**Above-the-Line Deductions (Adjustments to Income):**
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You paid $2,900, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500** per year. This is an "above-the-line" deduction, meaning you can take it even if you don't itemize. *Note: Because your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) will be around $80,000, your deduction may be partially phased out (the 2023 phase-out range for single filers is $75,000 to $90,000).*
*   **Self-Employment Tax Deduction:** You can deduct 50% of the self-employment taxes you pay on your $9,800 freelance income.

**Business Deductions (Schedule C - Freelance Work):**
*   **Unreimbursed Work Supplies ($420):** If these supplies were for your W-2 job, they are **no longer deductible** (the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act eliminated miscellaneous itemized deductions for W-2 employees). However, if these supplies were used for your freelance editing/proofreading work, you can deduct the full $420 as a business expense against your 1099-NEC income.

### 2. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**You should take the Standard Deduction.**
For 2023, the standard deduction for a single filer is **$13,850**. 
To itemize, your total itemized deductions would need to exceed $13,850. Your itemized deductions would consist of:
*   Charitable donation: $650
*   State and Local Taxes (SALT): Your MN state income taxes (likely around $4,000–$5,000)
Since your total itemized deductions would only be around $5,000–$6,000, it is much more beneficial to take the $13,850 standard deduction. *(Note: You cannot deduct the $650 charity donation if you take the standard deduction, as the temporary pandemic-era rule allowing a $300/$600 charitable deduction for non-itemizers expired).*

### 3. How to Treat the Home Office
Because you are a W-2 employee *and* a freelancer, you can **only** claim the home office deduction against your freelance (1099-NEC) income. You cannot use it to offset your W-2 income. 

Since your office is 180 sq ft and your apartment is 810 sq ft, your business use percentage is **22.22%**. You have two options:
*   **Simplified Method:** 180 sq ft × $5 = **$900 deduction**. (Easier, requires less record-keeping).
*   **Actual Expenses Method:** 22.22% of your actual expenses. Your rent alone is $23,400/year ($1,950 × 12). 22.22% of your rent is **$5,200**. You can also add 22.22% of your utilities, renter's insurance, and internet. 
**Recommendation:** The Actual Expenses method will give you a significantly higher deduction (over $5,200 compared to $900), which will greatly reduce your taxable freelance income.

### 4. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When filing, you will need the following IRS forms:
*   **Form 1040:** The main tax return document.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your freelance business income and claim your student loan interest deduction.
*   **Schedule 2:** To report your self-employment tax on your freelance income.
*   **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Required because your taxable interest ($1,880) is over the $1,500 threshold.
*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** To report your $9,800 in 1099-NEC income and deduct your home office and (if applicable) work supplies.
