Based on the details you've provided, I can help you break down your 2023 tax situation. Here is a comprehensive guide on what you can deduct, whether to itemize, how to handle your home office, and the forms you'll need.

### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**Recommendation: Take the Standard Deduction.**
For the 2023 tax year, the standard deduction for a single filer is **$13,850**. 
To itemize, your total eligible deductions would need to exceed $13,850. Your itemized deductions would primarily consist of your $650 charitable donation to Second Harvest Heartland and your state/local taxes (capped at $10,000). Combined, these are well under the $13,850 threshold, so taking the standard deduction will save you more money and time. *(Note: Because you are taking the standard deduction, the $650 charitable donation will unfortunately not provide a tax benefit for 2023).*

### 2. What You Can Deduct (Above-the-Line & Business Deductions)
Even with the standard deduction, you can still take "above-the-line" deductions and business deductions:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You paid $2,900, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500**. Additionally, because your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) will be around $83,000, you fall into the phase-out range for 2023 ($75,000 to $90,000 for single filers). This means your $2,500 maximum deduction will be partially reduced.
*   **Unreimbursed Work Supplies ($420):** 
    *   *If these were for your W-2 job at Summit Financial:* You **cannot** deduct them. The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act eliminated miscellaneous itemized deductions for W-2 employees.
    *   *If these were for your freelance editing/proofreading:* You **can** deduct them as a business expense against your 1099-NEC income.
*   **Self-Employment Tax Deduction:** You get to deduct 50% of the self-employment taxes you pay on your $9,800 freelance income as an adjustment to your gross income.

### 3. How to Treat the Home Office
Since you use your 180 sq ft office *exclusively* for your freelance business, you qualify for the Home Office Deduction against your 1099-NEC income. You have two ways to calculate this:

*   **Option A: The Simplified Method**
    *   Calculation: 180 sq ft × $5 per sq ft = **$900 deduction**.
    *   *Pros:* Very easy, requires less record-keeping.
*   **Option B: The Actual Expenses Method**
    *   Calculation: Your office is 22.22% of your apartment (180 sq ft / 810 sq ft). You can deduct 22.22% of your rent. 
    *   Rent deduction alone: $1,950 × 12 months = $23,400 total rent. 22.22% of $23,400 = **$5,200 deduction**.
    *   *Pros:* Yields a significantly higher deduction. You can also add 22.22% of your renter's insurance, utilities (electricity/gas), and internet bill.
    *   **Recommendation:** Use the **Actual Expenses Method**. It will drastically lower your taxable freelance income.

### 4. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When you file, make sure your tax software generates (or you manually fill out) the following federal forms:
*   **Form 1040:** Your main individual income tax return.
*   **Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income):** This is where your business income, student loan interest deduction, and self-employment tax deduction will be reported.
*   **Schedule 2 (Additional Taxes):** To report your self-employment tax.
*   **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Because your combined interest ($1,880) and dividends ($380) exceed $1,500, the IRS requires you to list the payers on this schedule.
*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** To report your $9,800 in 1099-NEC income and deduct your freelance expenses (like the $420 in supplies, if applicable).
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** You will need this form to calculate and claim your home office deduction using the Actual Expenses method. The final number flows onto your Schedule C.
*   **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** To calculate the Social Security and Medicare taxes owed on your net freelance profit.

*Don't forget your state taxes! You will also need to file a Minnesota state return (Form M1), which will start with your federal adjusted gross income.*

