To itemize, your deductible expenses (which would primarily be your $650 charitable donation to Second Harvest Heartland and your state/local income taxes) would need to exceed $13,850. Because Minnesota state taxes on your income will likely be around $3,500–$4,500, your total itemized deductions will fall well short of $13,850. 
**Recommendation:** You should take the **Standard Deduction**. Keep the receipt for your $650 donation for your records, but you will not need to itemize it.

### 2. Home Office Deduction
*Important Note:* You can only claim a home office deduction against your **freelance (1099-NEC) income**, not your W-2 wages. Because you use the 180 sq ft room *exclusively* for your office, you qualify.
* **Simplified Method:** 180 sq ft × $5 = $900 deduction.
* **Actual Expenses Method:** Your office is 22.22% of your apartment (180 sq ft / 810 sq ft). Your annual rent is $23,400 ($1,950 × 12 months). 22.22% of $23,400 is **$5,200**. You can also deduct 22.22% of your utilities and renter's insurance.
**Recommendation:** The Actual Expenses method gives you a significantly larger deduction ($5,200+ vs. $900) to offset your freelance income.

### 3. Unreimbursed Work Supplies ($420)
* If these supplies were for your **W-2 job** at Summit Financial, they are *no longer deductible* (this deduction was suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act).
* If they were for your **freelance work**, you can deduct the full $420 as a business expense to further lower your taxable freelance income.

### 4. Student Loan Interest ($2,900)
The IRS maximum deduction for student loan interest is **$2,500**. This is an "above-the-line" deduction (an adjustment to income), meaning you can claim it even if you take the standard deduction. 
*Note:* Because your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) will likely be around $77,000, you fall slightly into the 2023 phase-out range for single filers ($75,000 to $90,000). This means your $2,500 maximum deduction will be slightly reduced (prorated).

### 5. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When you file, make sure your tax software generates (or you manually fill out) the following forms:
* **Form 1040:** The main U.S. Individual Income Tax Return.
* **Schedule 1:** To report your net freelance business income and claim your student loan interest deduction.
* **Schedule 2:** To report the self-employment tax on your freelance income.
* **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Required because your taxable interest from Ally Bank ($1,880) is over the $1,500 IRS threshold.
* **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** To report your $9,800 in 1099-NEC income and deduct your freelance expenses (like the $420 in supplies).
* **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** To calculate and claim your $5,200+ home office deduction using the actual expenses method (this flows directly to Schedule C).
* **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** To calculate the Social Security and Medicare taxes on your net freelance profit.

